You are drafting an implementation plan as an ordered set of incremental vertical
deliverables, each expressed as user stories.

Before starting:
- Read the repo instructions, the specification tree, and the evaluation/planning
  protocol in that order.
- Confirm that Phase 1 (specification comprehension) and Phase 2 (technology choice)
  are complete. If either is not, do that first and stop — do not draft deliverables
  on an unapproved stack. If they are complete, state the approved stack and the plan
  location in one line so we agree on the basis.
- Then ask me any questions you have before starting the list of deliverables.

What a vertical deliverable is here:
- A dependency-closed set of capabilities that a person can see and operate through the
  production UI, traversing the real application/domain behaviour and the
  persistence/infrastructure those capabilities require. It is runnable and testable
  end to end.
- Layer-only work is forbidden without a specifically recorded, separately approved
  exception. Enabling or foundational work belongs inside the first vertical slice that
  uses it, never as a slice of its own.

The output I want:
1. An ordered, numbered list of deliverables.
2. Each deliverable carries 1–4 user stories, in the classic form:
   "As a _________, I would like _________, so that I can _________."
   Keep the count proportional to the size of the stories.
3. Each deliverable also carries:
   - a high-level description of the capability set it delivers;
   - the operations, mechanisms, seed/default additions, and durable-representation
     changes it introduces;
   - the acceptance scenarios it must satisfy, marked honestly as full or partial.
4. A "declared scope" line wherever a deliverable deliberately covers only part of a
   scenario, saying which later deliverable completes it.

Scope discipline:
- Be very selective about what goes into the foundation deliverable: the minimum needed
  to support the first real capability and nothing more.
- Add functionality to a foundational element only when the story being built requires
  it. Concretely: a selector offers only the values the current deliverable can actually
  deliver, and a later deliverable adds its own values when it needs them.
- Make each deliverable a small set of incremental functionality.
- Defer work to the deliverable that first needs it, rather than front-loading.

Ordering:
- Sequence by trader capability, not by module or layer.
- Place cross-cutting concerns as obligations inside the deliverable that touches them,
  not as deliverables. Migration is the model case: a durable-representation change is a
  task inside the deliverable that makes it.
- Put the product's signature capabilities as early as they can genuinely be built.
  Do not let a signature feature sit late merely because it is large. For this product,
  Daily Review and the ongoing risk/reward calculations are the cases I care about: they
  should follow plan creation and opening execution, and precede closing a position.
- Prefer manual/deferred approaches early where they are simpler for the trader, and
  introduce the harder automated machinery in the deliverable where it first becomes
  worth it.

Parallelism:
- Within a deliverable, note where domain/persistence work and UI work can proceed in
  parallel once that deliverable's interface is fixed.
- Across deliverables, note only genuinely safe pairs — those avoiding the shared
  resources that force serialization (schema, seed/default manifest, capability
  manifest, navigation shell, and any shared analysis operation).
- Name the shared resources that require a single owner at a time.

Not in this document:
- Task-level breakdowns. No describe/it-should cases, no test lists, no red→green
  procedures, no per-task evidence. We agree on this list first; I will then ask you for
  a detailed task breakdown for one or more deliverables.
- No scaffolding and no implementation code.

When the draft exists: present it for review one consequential decision at a time, lead
with a recommendation and the tradeoff, and record what we accept. Update the document
in place rather than restating it.
